  "nameStory": "The Hawthorn Mining Bee gets its name from its strong preference for visiting hawthorn flowers in spring, where females gather pollen and nectar to provision their underground nests. Like all mining bees, this species 'mines' tunnels in the ground, creating burrows that look like tiny volcano craters in bare soil. The scientific name 'crataegi' comes from Crataegus, the genus name for hawthorn trees.",
  "description": "The Hawthorn Mining Bee is a solitary ground-nesting bee that's about the size of a honeybee but more robust-looking. Females are typically dark brown to black with pale hair bands across their abdomen, while males tend to be slightly smaller and more golden-brown. Unlike social bees that live in hives, each female digs her own burrow in the soil where she raises her young alone. They're important early-season pollinators, especially for fruit trees and shrubs.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Ground nesting behavior",
      "description": "Look for small conical mounds of excavated soil with a pencil-sized hole in the center, often in bare or sparsely vegetated areas where females dig their burrows",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Hawthorn preference",
      "description": "Frequently seen visiting white hawthorn flowers in late spring, along with other members of the rose family like apple, cherry, and serviceberry blossoms",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Female appearance",
      "description": "Robust, dark-bodied bee with a fuzzy, almost velvety texture and pale grayish-white hair bands that create subtle stripes across the abdomen",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flight pattern",
      "description": "Strong, purposeful flight with a slightly buzzy sound, often seen hovering low over bare ground when searching for nesting sites",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Pollen baskets",
      "description": "Females carry dry, powdery pollen on specialized hairs on their hind legs, creating visible pale or yellowish 'pollen pants' when foraging",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in areas with well-drained, sandy or loamy soils where females can easily excavate burrows, often in gardens, orchards, woodland edges, and disturbed sites. They prefer locations near their preferred flowering plants, especially hawthorns and other members of the rose family. Look for them in areas with partial sun exposure and minimal ground cover vegetation.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Peak activity period from April through early June when adults emerge, mate, and females begin nesting. This is when you'll see them most actively visiting hawthorn and fruit tree blossoms.",
    "summer": "Activity decreases significantly by mid-June as adults complete their life cycle. New larvae are developing underground in provisioned cells but are not visible.",
    "fall": "No adult activity visible above ground. The next generation remains as larvae in underground cells, feeding on stored pollen provisions.",
    "winter": "Larvae overwinter in their underground burrows, pupating in late winter to emerge as adults the following spring."
  },
  "funFact": "A single female Hawthorn Mining Bee can move over 100 times her body weight in soil while excavating her burrow, creating a tunnel system up to 8 inches deep with multiple side chambers - each one stocked with a perfectly measured ball of pollen and nectar that will feed exactly one of her offspring through its entire development!",
  "confusionNotes": "Often confused with other dark mining bees in the Andrena genus, but the Hawthorn Mining Bee's strong preference for rose family flowers (especially hawthorns) and its relatively early spring emergence help distinguish it. Remember: 'Hawthorn helper in spring' - if you see a dark mining bee working hawthorn blossoms in May, it's likely this species.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Despite being called a 'mining' bee, these insects are beneficial pollinators that don't damage plants or structures - the name simply refers to their ground-nesting habits. They're sometimes mistaken for small bumblebees, but mining bees are much less fuzzy and have a more streamlined appearance.",
  "compareHints": "Among New England's many Andrena species, the Hawthorn Mining Bee is distinguished by its strong association with rose family flowers and its mid-spring emergence timing. While the Cherry Mining Bee (Andrena pruni) has similar plant preferences, it tends to be slightly smaller and emerges earlier when cherry trees bloom before hawthorns. The Hawthorn Mining Bee also has more distinct pale hair bands compared to many of its darker relatives in the genus.",
